PKG(1)                      User Commands                    PKG(1)

NAME
    pkg - install, remove, list and build packages

SYNOPSIS
    pkg [--root dir] [--arch machine] [--config file] [--keys dir] install file|name[-version]...
    pkg [--root dir] [--arch machine] [--config file] [--keys dir] check file|name[-version]...
    pkg [--root dir] [--config file] [--keys dir] update
    pkg [--root dir] [--config file] [--keys dir] search [pattern]
    pkg [--root dir] [--arch machine] [--config file] [--keys dir] upgrade [name...]
    pkg [--root dir] remove [--force] name...
    pkg [--root dir] list
    pkg [--root dir] info name|file
    pkg [--root dir] verify [name...]
    pkg [--root dir] perms
    pkg [--root dir] bootconfig
    pkg [--root dir] build dir [file]

DESCRIPTION
    pkg installs packages from package files and from signed
    repositories into the root filesystem and records every
    installed file with its mode, owner, size and SHA-256 digest. A
    package is a gzip compressed ustar archive named name-version.mpk
    whose first member is the manifest and whose files lie under files/,
    relative to the root, as in files/usr/bin/calc. Every check of an
    installation runs before anything is written: the dependencies, the
    conflicts, the shared libraries with their ABI numbers and symbols,
    and the ownership of every path. A file that exists already and
    belongs to no package is not replaced.

    An installation writes every file to a temporary name beside its
    target and renames the files into place only when all of them are
    written, which means that a failed installation leaves the installed
    files as they were. A file the manifest names with a config line is
    a configuration file: an upgrade replaces it only when it is
    unchanged since its installation, and otherwise writes the new
    version beside it as file.pkgnew. remove saves a changed
    configuration file as file.pkgsave.

    A repository is a directory served over plain HTTP that contains the
    archives, an index and the Ed25519 signature of the index. The index
    lists every archive with its name, version, dependencies, size and
    SHA-256 digest. Since HTTP offers no protection, pkg uses an index
    only when its signature verifies against a key in /etc/pkg/keys, and
    an archive only when its size and digest equal those in the index.

COMMANDS
    install   install or upgrade packages; an argument that is an
              existing file, contains a slash or ends in .mpk is a
              package file, any other names a package in the
              repositories, as name or name-version, and its
              dependencies are resolved through the indexes
    check     run the checks of install without writing anything; a
              package file is also compared with the entry of the same
              name and version in the indexes
    update    fetch the index of every repository, verify its signature
              and store it under /var/lib/pkg/_repos. A refused index
              leaves the previous one in place
    search    print the entries of the indexes whose name or summary
              contains pattern, one name, version, repository and
              summary per line
    upgrade   install the higher versions that the indexes offer for
              the installed packages, or for the named ones
    remove    remove installed packages; --force removes a package that
              other packages depend on
    list      print the installed packages, one name, version and
              summary per line
    info      print the manifest and the files of an installed package
              or of a package file
    verify    compare the recorded digest, mode and, for root, owner of
              every installed file with the file on disk. A changed
              configuration file is reported without counting as a
              failure
    perms     print every installed file and every directory the
              packages created as "path mode uid gid", the form mkfs -p
              reads
    bootconfig
              write /boot/limine.conf from the installed kernel package
              and /etc/kernel/cmdline, which an upgrade of the kernel
              package also does
    build     write a package from a directory with manifest and
              files/, with the arch and needs lines derived from the
              ELF files. The members belong to root and have the modes
              of the files

OPTIONS
    --root dir       install into the tree dir instead of the root
                     filesystem, and read the records, etc/pkg.conf
                     and etc/pkg/keys below it
    --arch machine   accept packages for machine instead of the machine
                     of the running system, for a tree prepared on
                     another system
    --config file    read the repositories from file instead of
                     /etc/pkg.conf
    --keys dir       read the trusted public keys from dir instead of
                     etc/pkg/keys below the root, for a target root
                     that has none yet

CONFIGURATION
    /etc/pkg.conf names the repositories, one per line, in the order in
    which they are searched, and may set the timeout in seconds for the
    connection and for every wait for data (30 by default):

        repo build http://10.0.2.2:8000/$arch
        timeout 30

    $arch in a URL is replaced with the machine name of the system.
    A URL of the form file:///path names a repository in the file
    system. Its index is read from there and verified as a download
    would be, and its archives are used in place.
    Index entries for another machine are not listed.

    A key file in /etc/pkg/keys has the suffix .pub and contains one
    line, ed25519 followed by the public key in hexadecimal. The image
    contains the key of the build as /etc/pkg/keys/build.pub.

EXIT STATUS
    pkg exits with 0 on success and 1 when an operation failed. A
    refused signature, an archive that differs from the index, a
    connection that fails or times out, an HTTP status other than 200
    and a response shorter or longer than its Content-Length are
    failures, and each prints a message naming the repository or the
    package. An unknown command or missing arguments exit with 2.

BUNDLED APPLICATIONS
    Optional applications are available as archives in /usr/share/packages.
    Install selected archives, or all of them, with the examples below.
    They are not installed automatically. Until the system is installed
    on a disk of its own, the root image is rebuilt with every build, and
    installed packages disappear with it. make repo writes the same
    archives as a signed repository to build/repo/ARCH on the host.

FILES
    /etc/pkg.conf                   the repositories
    /etc/pkg/keys/*.pub             the trusted public keys
    /var/lib/pkg/name/manifest      the manifest as installed
    /var/lib/pkg/name/files         mode, owner, size, SHA-256 and path of each file
    /var/lib/pkg/name/dirs          mode, owner and path of each directory created
    /var/lib/pkg/_repos/repo/       the verified index of a repository
    /var/lib/pkg/launcher           launcher entries of the packages
    /var/lib/pkg/mime.types         MIME types of the packages
    /var/lib/pkg/mime.apps          MIME handlers of the packages

EXAMPLES
    pkg install /usr/share/packages/calc-*.mpk
    pkg install /usr/share/packages/*.mpk
    pkg build pong
    pkg install pong-1.2.0.mpk
    pkg update
    pkg search calc
    pkg install calc
    pkg install calc-0.2.0
    pkg upgrade
    pkg check calc-0.2.0.mpk
    pkg list
    pkg remove pong

SEE ALSO
    http(1), tar(1), gzip(1), ld(1)
